//? Title: Gradient clipping: Explicit Masking, State Paths and Source Controls
//? Subtitle: Native recurrent tensor components and an editable source training loop
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The original gradient clipping experiment is retained with source fixtures, parameter initialization, full state histories and gradient controls. Reusable model math lives in datamunge; the complete C++ program below owns training, optimizer writes, checkpoints and diagnostic queries.
)
\toc

> Source experiment and explicit tensor workflow

The source uses a small synthetic single-marker sequence-memory task. These are mechanism experiments with the original short budgets, rather than real-data benchmark claims. Optional TensorFlow replay is skipped when its recordings are absent. The program preserves every recorded state, input/initial-state derivative, padding/causality control and source checkpoint.

> Read the state, mask and update separately

The same206-value tanh recurrence is fitted independently under no control, global norm.05 and coordinate value.01. Every policy retains the same initial weights, data and batch order. Plain SGD(.1), rather than Adam, performs48 writes per policy. Raw/applied gradients, scales and all source comparisons remain explicit.

The original pools contain512 fitting,128 validation and 128 test sequences, with twelve padded timesteps, two input channels, balanced alternating classes and a marker before the final valid token. Each ordinary fit uses Adam(.01),32-row batches and three epochs:48 updates. This report deliberately replaces Adam with the three source SGD policies described above. All synthetic draws and source IDs are recorded by the canonical generator.

The report introduces 206 learned values in the actual source graph. Valid lengths are structural inputs, while hidden/cell tensors and weights retain live derivative paths. Inference receives inputs, lengths and explicit initial states; targets belong to the objective only.

